4  # PLAN.md stage this tree is graded against. Stage 5 (actors, runtime,
   # @meta) has not arrived, so tests carrying `.stage 5` are deferred.
   #
   # 4 IS THE GRADE, NOT A CLAIM THAT 1-4 ARE FINISHED.
   #
   # THE BOARD IS GREEN ON BOTH TOOLCHAINS. As of 2026-08-11
   # `make test` is 464 / 0 against the bootstrapper and
   # `make test-zen` is 464 / 0 against the shipped compiler, with
   # the same four stage-5 tests deferred on each.
   #
   # IT WAS 451 / 2 FOR PART OF 2026-08-10, AND BOTH REDS WERE REAL:
   #
   #     must-fail/sema/match_arms_disagree     the program compiled;
   #                                            it must be rejected
   #     must-fail/sema/match_arm_paren_form    message substring not
   #                                            found
   #
   # THE SHIPPED COMPILER DID NOT TYPE-CHECK MATCH ARMS. This was a
   # silent wrong answer, not a missing message:
   #
   #     f = (b: bool) i32 { b.match({ true => 1, false => false }) }
   #
   # was accepted, emitted C that cc accepted, RAN, and printed 0. The
   # bootstrapper said `expected i32, found bool`. With a `str` arm
   # instead the emitted C returned a zg_str from an int32_t function
   # and cc rejected it -- so for that day the type checker was not
   # the authority on Zen's types, cc was, and only where the C types
   # happened to disagree.
   #
   # FIXED THE SAME DAY, and not in the join. The rule was already
   # written down IN THE FILE THAT BROKE IT -- src/sema/sema_join.zen's
   # header: "two arms of genuinely different types mean something the
   # language cannot express -- inventing a join for it would hide the
   # mistake rather than report it." But the join has no expectation
   # to name, so the diagnostic lives in `check_arms`
   # (src/sema/sema_match.zen), which puts every arm where the match
   # itself stands, at the two positions that wrote a type down: a
   # function's tail and a binding with a written type. Three more
   # must-fail tests pin the same question at a field, an argument
   # and an element.
   #
   # AND FOUR POSITIONS THAT RULE COULD NOT REACH, closed 2026-08-16.
   # `check_arms` needs a position with a WORD. Where nothing decides
   # the type it asked nothing at all, and each of these compiled, ran,
   # and printed the same plausible 0:
   #
   #     x = b.match({ true => 1, false => false });  an untyped binding
   #     println("{}", b.match({ .. }))               the print sugar
   #     b.match({ .. }) + 1                          an operand
   #     id(b.match({ .. }))                          a parameter `T`
   #
   # The last is the instructive one: a type parameter DID write a word
   # down, and `assignable` says every arm reaches it, so the word
   # decided nothing and the silence was indistinguishable from
   # agreement.
   #
   # THE ARMS STILL OWE EACH OTHER. `sema_hoist.check_arms_agree` holds
   # them to their own join, and `SemaFault.ArmsDisagree` says it in the
   # ARMS' voice -- "expected int" would put the first arm's literal
   # type in the voice of something that asked for it, and nothing did.
   # `bootstrap/sema._arms_agree` is the same rule and the same
   # sentence, because a must-fail expectation is read by both
   # compilers and a message only one of them writes is a red board.
   #
   # AND THE JOIN HAD BEEN ANSWERING THE WRONG TYPE, at twenty-eight
   # matches in src/. `(n == 0).match({ true => 1, false => n })` over a
   # `usize` is `usize`; first-arm-wins answered `int`, the literal's
   # own type. Nothing ever complained, because `assignable` lets an
   # `int` literal reach every integer type -- the wrong answer was also
   # a lenient one, and it stayed invisible until something asked the
   # arms to agree. `sema_join.settled_literal` is the fix and the guard
   # beside it is the reason it is not a supertype: never settle a
   # literal into a slot nobody has computed.
   #
   # HOW IT SURVIVED EVERY ORACLE, which is the reusable part: a corpus
   # test is a VALID program, so none would ever contain mismatched
   # arms; the differential oracle compares two compilers only on
   # programs somebody wrote down; and no must-fail test covered arms
   # until one was migrated in on 2026-08-10. A gap nothing exercises
   # is invisible to a differential harness -- being wrong in only one
   # implementation is not enough, something has to ASK.
   #
   # Read the green board as "nothing we thought to check is broken",
   # never as "stage 4 is done", because the gap between those two
   # sentences is where every silent bug in this tree has lived.
   #
   # Measured, not asserted, and all of it below the grade:
   #
   #   - The LSP answers hover, lexical semanticTokens and the
   #     lifecycle, and PUBLISHES DIAGNOSTICS -- all three phases, one
   #     notification per file, an empty list when the errors are gone
   #     (corpus/lsp/diagnostics_publish_and_clear). Hover answers 9 of
   #     12 probed positions on a file that imports something -- the
   #     import line, a space and a brace stay null, gated by
   #     corpus/lsp/hover_answers_an_imported_name. Everything else is
   #     still refused by name with -32601.
   #   - Stage 2's formatter models the FILE and not the DECLARATION. It
   #     normalises bytes, so `make fmt` is green; it does not know a
   #     declaration from a comment from a string literal.
   #   - Struct field defaults were specified twice in DESIGN.md and
   #     worked nowhere, for weeks, in both implementations. FIXED
   #     2026-08-08; the entry stays because of HOW it hid.
   #
   # That last one is the pattern, not an anecdote, and the first
   # diagnosis of it was wrong in an instructive way. "The compiler
   # never eats this feature" was the guess. It ate it: hash.zen
   # declares `state :: u64 = 5381` -- the hasher's SEED -- and had been
   # starting at 0, and parser.zen:167,172 were zeroed too.
   #
   # It survived because THE WRONG ANSWER WAS A PLAUSIBLE ANSWER. A hash
   # seeded at 0 still hashes. Every other default in the tree was
   # already zero, so the bug's output was indistinguishable from the
   # intent. Nothing crashed, so nothing looked -- and a compiler that
   # miscompiles a feature identically at every stage still reaches a
   # byte-identical fixpoint.
   #
   # So the audit question is not "does it build?" and not "does `src/`
   # use this?". It is: WHAT DOES THIS FEATURE'S FAILURE LOOK LIKE, AND
   # WOULD ANYONE NOTICE? Where the answer is "a plausible value", assume
   # it is broken until a test with a non-zero, non-default-looking
   # expectation says otherwise.
   #
   # Lowering this number would defer the tests that are meant to fail
   # and make the board read better without a line of work being done,
   # which is the one thing it must not do.
